WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:05.100
Stephen King delves deep into the psyches
and lives of even his most marginal characters,

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.040
which can make adaptations of his stories
into a visual medium pretty tricky.

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:12.900
His magnum opus, It, has been brought to both
TV and the big screen.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.500
How do the casts stack up?

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:15.940
Float on down and see.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:20.640
Which came first: the fear of clowns, or Stephen
King's monstrous Pennywise?

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.870
At this point it almost doesn't matter, since
the two are so inextricably linked in the

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:28.220
global consciousness and the nightmares of
children and adults alike.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:33.360
During his first encounter with Georgie Denbrough
in the sewer in 1957, Pennywise has yellow

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.200
eyes that turn blue as he talks.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:36.000
King writes,

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.019
"The face of the clown in the stormdrain was
white, there were funny tufts of red hair

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:44.180
on either side of his bald head, and there
was a big clown-like smile painted over his

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:44.680
mouth.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:49.080
If George had been inhabiting a later year,
he would have surely thought of Ronald McDonald."

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:49.980
King goes further:

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:53.420
"He was wearing a baggy silk suit with great
big orange buttons.

00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:57.809
A bright tie, electric-blue, flopped down
his front, and on his hands were big white

00:00:57.809 --> 00:01:00.820
gloves, like the kind Mickey Mouse and Donald
Duck always wore."

00:01:00.949 --> 00:01:05.860
When Ben Hanscom sees the clown shortly after,
he is also wearing a "floppy yellow bow-tie"

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:08.510
and notices that "the clown cast no shadow."

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:13.780
25 years later, when Pennywise attacks Adrian
Mellon under the bridge, King reiterates earlier

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:18.530
Bozo and Ronald McDonald comparisons, but
adds that "later consideration had caused

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:20.420
him to think the clown really looked like
neither."

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.860
King also describes "great big teeth" like
those of a circus lion.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.020
Those definitely made it into the films.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:36.440
Also known as "Big Bill" and "Stuttering Bill,"
even to his friends, the unofficial leader

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:40.140
of the Losers Club is described not with the
dark brown hair he has in the film, but as

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.480
having bright red hair and blue eyes.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:46.700
After the first attack on Pennywise in the
house on Neibolt Street, Bill gives his shirt

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:47.340
to Beverly.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:51.620
King writes that, when shirtless, Bill had
"a narrow chest, the visible track of his

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:53.920
ribs, [and] sunburned freckled shoulders."

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.450
Even when they're young, Richie Tozier muses
that John F. Kennedy reminds him of Bill.

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:02.560
Older Bill, as depicted in his author photo
on the back of one of his novels, is described

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.280
as "a bald man wearing glasses."

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.400
King writes that "he is tall, and has a certain
presence."

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:10.780
Bill is "already balding, already inclining
a bit toward fat.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:14.590
He speaks slowly in company, and at times
seems nearly inarticulate."

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:15.970
Another description reads:

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:19.600
"His eyes, magnified by the lenses of his
glasses, looked thoughtful."

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:24.640
That part lines up with how older Bill appears
in the 1990 It miniseries, played by Richard

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:25.140
Thomas.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.720
But what doesn't fit is, quote, "that red,
fine hair that he had begun to lose when he

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:31.030
was only a college sophomore."

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:35.069
Thomas' version of the character not only
has hair, but enough of it to pull back into

00:02:35.069 --> 00:02:36.060
a ponytail.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:41.520
While Pennywise the Clown had many, many victims
detailed in the pages of King's It, the first

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.180
we properly meet is Bill's little brother
Georgie.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:48.500
His physical description is notably sparse
- all we know at first is that Georgie is

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:52.980
"a small boy in a yellow slicker and red galoshes,"
and he is just six years old.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:57.540
Much later in the story, as Pennywise's campaign
of terror begins ramping up against the Losers

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:01.640
in earnest, King describes Georgie in more
detail from a family photo album.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:04.160
"In [the picture] George was wearing a crew-neck
shirt.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.500
His fly-away hair was slicked down with water.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:10.670
He was grinning, revealing two empty slots
in which new teeth would never grow."

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:15.270
As Pennywise uses Georgie's ghost to taunt
Bill, he appears with his yellow slicker covered

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:17.260
in blood, as well as his arm missing.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:21.840
Both movie adaptations of It end up faithfully
portraying Bill's little brother, which was

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.680
easy to do since he's described so ambiguously.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:33.640
Like Bill, Beverly has bright red hair that
King's novel describes as auburn, coppery,

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:38.080
and deep red, sometimes with blonde highlights,
the "dim Irish fire of her hair."

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:42.340
As a young girl her hair was shoulder length,
and she wore it pulled back in a ponytail

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:43.200
most of the time.

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:48.380
Beverly has, quote, "gray-blue eyes, naturally
red lips, [and] creamy unblemished child's

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:51.320
skin," with "a tiny spray of freckles across
her nose."

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:56.420
As a child, her wardrobe is described as cheap
sweaters, penny loafers, and ill-fitting tartan

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:59.989
skirts that "probably came from the Salvation
Army thrift-box."

00:03:59.989 --> 00:04:04.370
Beverly often has huge bruises on her face
and arms - the signature of her father's abusive

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:04.920
handiwork.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:09.940
As an adult, Beverly is described as a "gorgeous
woman" with a slim but well-endowed figure.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:14.580
She's grown her red hair down to her waist
and her fingernails are "kept neat but brutally

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:15.080
short."

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:19.380
Shades of these descriptors are evident in
all of Beverly's portrayals on screen, except

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:20.380
for the short nails.

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:25.880
As a young man, Mike Hanlon is described as
"slim and well built" as well as "fast and

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:29.380
agile," factors that had saved him from several
beatings at Henry Bowers' hands.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:34.340
Mike has a "tight cap of hair," and often
wears "corduroys, a tee-shirt, and black high-topped

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:34.860
Keds."

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.940
Bill's assessment of Mike when he returns
to Derry reveals the extended trauma Mike

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.340
has experienced by remaining in the haunted
town.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:45.300
"[Bill] remembered a boy who had been about
five feet three, trim, and agile.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:47.920
Before him was a man who stood about five-seven.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:48.640
He was skinny.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:50.260
His clothes seemed to hang on him.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:54.820
And the lines in his face said he was on the
darker side of forty instead of only thirty-eight

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:55.320
or so."

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:59.780
Mike also wears glasses as an adult, and he
has a scar across his chest from when the

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:02.640
werewolf form of Pennywise scratched him at
Neibolt Street.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:07.920
His portrayal on screen in the 1990 miniseries
is truer to the book than the Muschietti movies,

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:12.561
where Chapter One gives much of his motivation
to Ben Hanscom and Chapter Two has him hit

00:05:12.561 --> 00:05:14.140
harder by the trauma of his youth.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:19.440
As a child, Ben Hanscom was described as larger
than any of the It adaptations have shown.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:23.630
King's detailed description paints him as
"an eleven-year-old kid with a can roughly

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:25.080
the size of New Mexico."

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:30.150
The boys at school bully him for his weight,
and King writes that Ben had four baggy sweatshirts

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:34.880
in which to drown his 210-pound body; one
brown, one green, and two blue.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:37.440
Older Ben couldn't be more the opposite of
his younger self.

00:05:37.790 --> 00:05:42.260
King writes that Ben is "lanky, sunburned,
dressed in a chambray shirt, faded jeans,

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:46.180
and a pair of scuffed engineer boots," with
"faint squint lines around the corners of

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:47.700
his eyes, but nowhere else."

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:50.860
Basically, somewhere between Clint Eastwood
and a Wrangler commercial.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:54.260
"Wrangler, the authentic Western jeans."

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:58.639
In contrast to his fellow Losers who wear
the psychological tolls of their trauma on

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:03.380
their faces, Ben looks "perhaps ten years
younger than his actual age, which was 38."

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:06.420
When the grown-up Losers meet in Derry for
dinner, King describes:

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:07.920
"Ben had gotten thin.

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:11.320
[His] clothes clung to a body which was slim
and narrow-hipped.

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:15.820
He wore a bracelet with heavy links on one
wrist - not gold links but copper ones."

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:20.510
Jay Ryan as older Ben in Chapter Two fits
this bill far more than John Ritter did in

00:06:20.510 --> 00:06:21.940
the 1990 miniseries.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:24.700
"Any spare change, mister?"

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:30.060
King's descriptions of young Stanley don't
give even a small hint as to how tragically

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:31.200
his story will end.

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:35.060
If anything, he initially seems like one of
the most likely characters to make it through

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:36.420
the battle with Pennywise.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:37.200
King writes,

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:42.880
"As always he looked small, slim, and preternaturally
neat - much too neat for a kid who was barely

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:43.380
eleven.

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:46.000
[...] He looked instead like the world's smallest
adult.

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:48.240
Then he smiled, and the illusion was broken."

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:52.440
Stan often wears a slicker, and carries his
bird book and binoculars with him.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:57.160
In the brief time we have with older Stan
before he takes his last bath, his wife describes

00:06:57.160 --> 00:07:01.850
him as "ropy with muscle" with "a hipshot
way of walking, a taste for bell-bottomed

00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:06.170
jeans on his days off, and the last ghosts
of adolescent acne still on his face."

00:07:06.170 --> 00:07:11.100
He keeps his black hair short and, like many
of the other Losers, wears glasses as an adult.

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:15.820
There are shades of this version in Andy Bean's
Stanley in It: Chapter Two, but almost none

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:17.720
at all in the 1990 miniseries.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:23.020
Like Georgie Denbrough, the physical description
of Richie Tozier is surprisingly bare in the

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:25.920
It novel, considering he's one of the key
seven characters.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:26.700
King writes,

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:29.060
"Tozier was a scrawny kid who wore glasses.

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:33.700
[...] His magnified eyes swam behind the thick
lenses with an expression of perpetual surprise.

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:37.560
He also had huge front teeth that earned him
the nickname Bucky Beaver."

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:41.620
Even older Ritchie only reflects on his appearance
about his younger self when he looks in the

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:42.120
mirror:

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:46.420
"He saw a boy who wore glasses, a thin boy
with a pale face that had somehow seemed to

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:47.300
scream Hit me!

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:48.300
Go on and hit me!

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:52.590
… Here are my eyes so blue and magnified
behind these hateful, hateful glasses, these

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:56.210
horn-rimmed specs one bow of which is held
on with adhesive tape."

00:07:56.210 --> 00:08:00.759
Finn Wolfhard's glasses in It: Chapter 1 look
a lot like that description, except for the

00:08:00.759 --> 00:08:04.940
little detail of the tape holding them together
- a crucial reminder of exactly how much he

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:06.620
suffers at the hands of Henry Bowers.

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:11.940
Older Richie trades his glasses for contacts,
until he returns to Derry and Pennywise starts

00:08:11.949 --> 00:08:13.110
messing with his eyes.

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:17.390
But by then he's no longer the odd duck out
of the Losers Club with specs; most of the

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:18.960
other Losers wear glasses too.

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:23.420
Thanks to his mother's domineering attitude
that constantly has Eddie in and out of the

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:28.670
emergency room, the young boy's face is described
as "small and pinched and worried - an old

00:08:28.670 --> 00:08:29.480
man's face."

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:31.800
Aging doesn't do Eddie many favors at all.

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:33.500
King describes adult Eddie as:

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:36.320
"...a short man with a timid, rabbity sort
of face.

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:40.840
Most of his hair was gone; what was left grew
in listless, piebald patches [...] there was

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:42.750
a little snow on Eddie's mountain already."

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:46.900
When they see each other again as grown-ups,
Big Bill's assessment of Eddie is just as

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:47.400
harsh.

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:51.180
"Eddie… had grown up to look quite a little
bit like Anthony Perkins.

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:55.320
[...] His hair was short, worn in an out-of-date
style that had been known as Ivy League in

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:57.100
the late fifties and early sixties.

00:08:57.260 --> 00:09:01.740
He was wearing a loud checked sportcoat [...] but
the watch on one wrist was Patek Philippe,

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:04.480
and the ring on the little finger of his right
hand was a ruby.

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:08.830
The stone was too hugely vulgar and too ostentatious
to be anything but real."

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:12.400
In short, Eddie on screen has never looked
much like the character on the page.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:15.080
Pennywise was not the Losers' only nemesis.

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:19.180
That unfortunate honor also goes to Henry
Bowers, about whom King writes,

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:21.360
"Henry was a big boy even for twelve.

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:23.740
His arms and legs were thick with farm muscle.

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:27.860
[...] Henry put in at least thirty hours a
week hoeing, weeding, planting, digging rocks,

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:29.880
cutting wood, and reaping."

00:09:30.460 --> 00:09:31.160
He continues:

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:35.280
"Henry's hair was cut in an angry-looking
flattop short enough for the white of his

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:36.280
scalp to show through.

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:40.820
He Butch-Waxed the front with a tube he always
carried in the hip pocket of his jeans, and

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:45.380
as a result the hair just above his forehead
looked like the teeth of an oncoming power-mower."

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:50.220
Interestingly, Bowers typically wears "a pink
motorcycle jacket with an eagle on the back"

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:54.420
- a pink jacket that nobody ever mentions
because the one person who did got beat so

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:56.560
badly Henry put him in the hospital.

00:09:56.680 --> 00:10:00.340
Henry has black eyes that sparkle, especially
when committing violence.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:02.360
"I'll kill you all.”

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:04.760
“Get some new material, champ."

00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:09.560
Years later, after spending most of his adult
life in a psychiatric hospital, Bowers had

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:14.000
put on weight, and his skin had, quote, "an
unhealthy tallowy hue; sagging."

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:19.420
In the 1990 adaptation, Henry's hair has turned
white from the trauma of encountering Pennywise.

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:24.540
But in the Muschietti adaptations, older Henry
does resemble the description in King's novel.

